	deps-bundle = https://github.com/slartibardfast/ds-lite-punch/releases/download/deps-vendor-v1/deps-vendor.tar.gz 7341bc06d101ebea32736db1d2d333af3d3b051a44590a521294b985b5e29730
# The toolchain is a digest-pinned container rather than a target triple:
# `--verify-build` runs the recorded `build` inside it, so the image is what
# reproduces the artifact and a movable tag would invalidate every hash
# recorded under it. The hash above was produced inside that image for the
# tagged commit by the host's own release, which stages the bundle below and
# builds with the network off. The component's artifact lanes build that same
# recipe, so a release asset hashes to the value above and a deployment takes
# those bytes rather than a local build's (call/0032). The lanes had built from
# the registry instead, which hashes differently, and the v0.4.0 release
# published such a build; they were corrected in the component on 2026-09-28
# (plan/0012's results). The
# bundle that `deps-bundle` names is the vendored dependency layer the offline
# build stages, and its bytes are not reproducible: the digest anchors the
# published asset, so a rebuild of the bundle will not match it. A
# version bump moves those bytes from 0.2.0 on: `--version` prints the crate
# version, so the string is compiled into the binary, and the pin therefore
# names the commit that carries the bump, which is also the commit the tag
# points at (call/0036). A comment line moves those bytes as well: Rust embeds
# the source position of every panicking call site, so the lines a comment
# occupies reach the binary and a comments-only pass still owes its release.
# To prove a pass moved no code, blank its comments in place rather than delete
# them, which rebuilds the pre-pass hash exactly.
#
# A container runtime arrived on this host on 2026-09-28 (`podman`), so the
# release that pinned the hash above ran here, in the recorded image, with the
# bundle staged and the network off. The host's own target/ still builds with
# the ambient compiler and hashes differently, which is a property of that
# build and not a defect in the record: a local build is for iteration, and the
# binary on the test router is the lane's artifact of the release before this
# one, checked against that release's hash before it was started.
